      "worked": "Both sides are defined when $\\sin\\theta\\ne0$ and $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$. On this domain, $1+\\cos\\theta\\ne0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sec\\theta+1}{\\sec\\theta-1}}&=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\cos\\theta}{1-\\cos\\theta}}\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sec\\theta+1}{\\sec\\theta-1}}&=\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\cos\\theta)^2}{(1-\\cos\\theta)(1+\\cos\\theta)}}\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sec\\theta+1}{\\sec\\theta-1}}&=\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\cos\\theta)^2}{1-\\cos^2\\theta}}\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sec\\theta+1}{\\sec\\theta-1}}&=\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\cos\\theta)^2}{\\sin^2\\theta}}\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sec\\theta+1}{\\sec\\theta-1}}&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1+\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}\\right)^2}\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sec\\theta+1}{\\sec\\theta-1}}&=\\left|\\frac{1+\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}\\right|\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sec\\theta+1}{\\sec\\theta-1}}&=\\left|\\operatorname{cosec}\\theta+\\cot\\theta\\right|.\n\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe principal square root gives an absolute value because $\\sqrt{u^2}=|u|$.",
